<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fibonacci</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1170–c. 1250</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Leonardo of Pisa, Leonardo Fibonacci</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fibonacci, or Leonardo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, was an Italian mathematician who learned Arabic mathematics as a boy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Bugia</a> (Béjaïa) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where his father served as a Pisan customs official. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liber-abaci">Liber Abaci</a> of 1202 introduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>–Arabic numerals and their methods of calculation to European merchants. The Fibonacci sequence takes its name from a problem about breeding rabbits in that book.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fibonacci.jpg" alt="Fibonacci" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Taty2007 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leonardo_Fibonacci.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Leonardo Fibonacci</b>’s Liber Abaci (1202) taught merchants Hindu-Arabic arithmetic, Italian merchants developed double-entry bookkeeping, set out in print by Luca Pacioli in 1494, and Florentine humanists such as Leonardo Bruni praised their republic’s liberty as the source of its greatness, an outlook the historian Hans Baron called civic…</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>The Indian numerals that al-Khwarizmi had explained reached Europe as “Arabic numerals”, commended to merchants by <b>Leonardo of Pisa</b> in his Liber Abaci of 1202.</blockquote><a href="/essays/cross-cultural-exchange">Cross-Cultural Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Its merchants brought back Arabic learning: <b>Leonardo Fibonacci's</b> Liber Abaci of 1202 introduced Hindu-Arabic numerals to Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-pisa">Republic of Pisa</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.72,10.4&amp;year=1222" aria-label="Pisa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4800 614 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Fibonacci"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5250 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5250 884h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.72,10.4&amp;year=1222">What was here in 1222? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
